Here's the full audit of `SerializerTests.cs`.

---

## Summary

**8 tests reviewed → 4 Critical, 1 High, 1 Medium, 0 Low**

This suite has a systemic problem: **half the tests use tautological or self-referential assertions** that can never fail regardless of whether the production code is correct. Coverage numbers look fine because every method is called, but the assertions prove nothing.

---

## Critical Findings

### 1. `NormalizeName_AlreadyNormalized_NoChange` — Tautological input (line 37–42)

`NameNormalizer.Normalize()` does `Trim().ToLowerInvariant()`. The test passes in `"alice"` — already lowercase, no whitespace — so the output trivially equals the input. The assertion can never fail.

```csharp
// BEFORE — always passes, proves nothing
var name = "alice";
var result = NameNormalizer.Normalize(name);
Assert.AreEqual(name, result);

// AFTER — verifies the transformation actually happens
var result = NameNormalizer.Normalize("  ALICE  ");
Assert.AreEqual("alice", result);
```

---

### 2. `UpperCase_ToUpper_Works` — Tautological input (line 71–77)

Calls `ToUpper()` on `"HELLO"`, which is already uppercase. The assertion `input == result` is trivially true.

```csharp
// BEFORE — identity operation, proves nothing
var input = "HELLO";
var result = input.ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual(input, result);

// AFTER — verifies the transformation
var result = "hello".ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual("HELLO", result);
```

---

### 3. `GetDisplayName_ReturnsFormattedName` — Self-referential assertion (line 55–61)

`GetDisplayName()` returns `Name` (identity). The test asserts `user.Name == display`, i.e. it asserts a property equals itself via the trivial getter. The name says "formatted" but no formatting occurs — and the test could never catch it if formatting were added or broken.

```csharp
// BEFORE — asserts output == input field, tautological
var user = new UserDto { Name = "Alice", Age = 30 };
var display = user.GetDisplayName();
Assert.AreEqual(user.Name, display);

// AFTER — assert against an independent expected value
var user = new UserDto { Name = "Alice", Age = 30 };
var display = user.GetDisplayName();
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", display);
// If GetDisplayName() is *supposed* to format (e.g., "Alice (30)"),
// update production code and assert that format here.
```

---

### 4. `MapToDto_MapBack_RoundTrip` — Lossy round-trip masking data loss (line 45–53)

The mapping is **inherently lossy**: `ToDto` drops `Id` and `Email`; `ToEntity` drops `Age`. Asserting round-trip equality on the lost fields (`Id=1→0`, `Email="a@b.com"→""`) either **fails silently** (if not actually run) or proves the test is broken. A round-trip test on a lossy mapping is the wrong pattern.

```csharp
// BEFORE — asserts round-trip on a lossy mapping
var entity = new UserEntity { Id = 1, FullName = "Alice Smith", Email = "a@b.com" };
var dto = Mapper.ToDto(entity);
var backToEntity = Mapper.ToEntity(dto);
Assert.AreEqual(entity.Id, backToEntity.Id);        // 1 != 0 — should fail!
Assert.AreEqual(entity.FullName, backToEntity.FullName);
Assert.AreEqual(entity.Email, backToEntity.Email);   // "a@b.com" != "" — should fail!

// AFTER — test forward mapping properties independently
[TestMethod]
public void ToDto_MapsFullNameToName()
{
    var entity = new UserEntity { Id = 1, FullName = "Alice Smith", Email = "a@b.com" };
    var dto = Mapper.ToDto(entity);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", dto.Name);
    Assert.AreEqual(0, dto.Age);   // Age is not mapped — document that
}

[TestMethod]
public void ToEntity_MapsNameToFullName()
{
    var dto = new UserDto { Name = "Alice Smith", Age = 30 };
    var entity = Mapper.ToEntity(dto);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", entity.FullName);
    Assert.AreEqual(0, entity.Id);      // Id is not mapped
    Assert.AreEqual("", entity.Email);  // Email is not mapped
}
```

---

## High Finding

### 5. `Validate_ValidInput_ReturnsSameInput` — Tests only the uninteresting path (line 63–69)

`Validator.ValidateEmail` throws on invalid input and returns the input on valid input. This test only checks the pass-through happy path. The critical behavior — rejection of bad emails — is untested, giving false confidence that validation works.

```csharp
// ADD — the tests that actually matter
[TestMethod]
public void ValidateEmail_NullOrEmpty_Throws()
{
    Assert.ThrowsException<ArgumentException>(() => Validator.ValidateEmail(""));
}

[TestMethod]
public void ValidateEmail_MissingAtSign_Throws()
{
    Assert.ThrowsException<ArgumentException>(() => Validator.ValidateEmail("not-an-email"));
}
```

---

## Medium Finding

